{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nids={'hyp-20261005-001-r4s0','hyp-20261005-001-r6s0','hyp-20261005-002-r10s1','hyp-20261005-001-r11s0','hyp-20261005-002-r8s1'}\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n e=json.loads(line)\n if e['id'] in ids:\n  print(e['id'],e['title'])\n  h=e.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261005-001-r4s0 Shorten EX operand delivery with registered dependency matches\nMake one micro optimization to EX operand delivery: compute register\ndependency matches before the ID/EX edge and keep the register-file\noperand boundary in ordinary flip-flops. Retain the pipeline topology,\ninstruction acceptance schedule and all arithmetic latencies. This\nis a timing-oriented implementation of the existing operand network,\nwith no predictor, cache, issue-width change or new execution stage.\n\nFor each raw fetched source address, compare in decode against the\ncurrent ID/EX rd and current EX/MEM rd, excluding destination zero.\nRegister the four raw match bits alongside ID/EX advancement: these\nproducers become EX/MEM and MEM/WB respectively on that same edge.\nReset or an allowed ID flush clears all match bits; genuine ID holds\nretain them. Use exactly ID's existing stall/flush priorities, so a\nload-use bubble clears matches while dmem or divider holds preserve\nthe live instruction's cohort. Compare raw source addresses independent\nof redirect-derived fetch validity, preserving the prior removal of\nredirect feedback from decode and hazard payload paths.\n\nIn EX, qualify the registered EX match with the CURRENT EX/MEM\nctrl.reg_write and the registered WB match with the CURRENT MEM/WB\nctrl.reg_write. Then preserve the existing EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB-over-\nstored-operand priority and 00/01/10 selector encodings. Do not latch\nreg_write prematurely: current controls already reflect branch/jump\nand memory alignment traps and divider bubbles. Do not gate MEM/WB\nforwarding with valid or WB w_en: a dmem stall clears valid but retains\ndata, destination and reg_write for forwarding to held EX consumers.\nNo destination equality comparison remains on the EX forwarding path.\n\nImplement x1 through x31 as generated, individually declared 32-bit\nflip-flop registers with constant per-word write destinations and\ndecoded write enables. Tie x0 to zero and select both combinational\nread ports from the register outputs. Keep deterministic syn\n…[19565 of 22637 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:53ef39be45125247]…\nALU results and all eight\nexact ALTOPS expressions. Preserve the dedicated LSU address sum,\nMEM multiplier, blocking divider and divider metadata. RVFI source\nvalues remain architectural forwarded operands, including AUIPC's\nexisting source-field convention. Preserve alignment traps, decoder\nlegality, x0, bus handshakes, all hold/flush priorities, retained WB\nforwarding, nret=1 ports and exactly-once ordered retirement.\n\nThe intended benefit is removal of one operand-wide selection level\nand its AUIPC control fanout ahead of the ordinary EX datapath. The\nforecast is an unmeasured eight percent fitness gain with medium\nconfidence. Result-case routing and the competing branch-recovery\npath may limit improvement. At unchanged cycles, acceptance requires\nmedian Gowin Fmax above 94.65254 MHz and fitness above 209.05356.\nRequire existing real/ALTOPS unit tests, formal, retirement cosim and\ncanonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker gates without weakening\nassertions, and measure all three vendor placement options.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
