{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_64","type":"agent_message","text":"The local clock-only prototypes tested below the champion. I’m switching the final hypothesis to a small fixed-count shift path: complete immediate shifts by one or two bits in EX while retaining MEM completion for the other shifts. This offers a way to recover dependency cycles without adding a full barrel shifter to EX."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_65", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml, json\nfrom jsonschema import validate\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-001-r14s0.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-001-r14s0',\n'title':'Early completion for one-bit and two-bit immediate shifts',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The r12s1 champion scores 224.09 iter/sec at 98.964 MHz and 4,416,239 marked cycles. It moved every shift to MEM to remove the five-level barrel shifter from the forwarded EX path, at the cost of shift-use bubbles. Immediate shifts by one or two bits need only fixed wiring and a single count-selection level. Returning just those operations to early completion can recover cycles while keeping general shifts off EX. LESSONS.md is absent. The micro_opt history, including r8s0's shared right shifter, r11s0's operand rewrite and r13s0's failed carry-lookahead change, was reviewed. Isolated local timing prototypes in this turn also failed to beat the champion, so a further clock-only mux or carry rewrite is less promising than this narrow shift fast path.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Add one local fixed-count completion path for legal SLLI, SRLI and SRAI whose encoded shamt is exactly 1 or 2. Keep the pipeline topology, retirement boundary and general late-shift implementation. This is one micro optimization to the existing shift execution policy, rather than restoring a variable barrel shifter or adding a stage.\n\nDefine fast_shift in EX from in.valid, \"'!in.ctrl.is_illegal, in.ctrl.alu_src, an ALU_SLL/SRL/SRA operation, and in.instr[24:20] equal to 5'\"'d1 or 5'd2. Derive the same classification from ID/EX in core.sv for the existing shift-use interlock. All register shifts, all other immediate counts, and invalid or illegal instructions retain their existing behavior. In particular, a register shift whose runtime count happens to equal 1 or 2 remains late.\n\nExtend alu.sv with a default-disabled fixed-shift parameter for the EX instance only. When ENABLE_SHIFTS is false and the fixed path is enabled, construct left results from {a[30:0],1'b0} and {a[29:0],2'b00}; construct right results from {fill,a[31:1]} and {{2{fill}},a[31:2]}, with fill = (op == ALU_SRA) && a[31]. Select between counts 1 and 2 using the registered immediate count, through at most one data selection level. Use the existing shift arms of the ALU result case, rather than appending a new mux after the completed ordinary ALU result. The selected ALU a and b remain fully forwarded and retain the existing AUIPC/immediate routing. For fast shifts, b is the decoded immediate, so its low five bits are the exact validated count. The partial EX shift result for any non-fast shift is deliberately provisional and must never become an architectural or early-forwarded result.\n\nCapture a fast_shift bit in EX/MEM alongside the owning instruction. Include it in normal payload and divider metadata initialization, reset it deterministically, retain it on exactly the existing dmem hold and clear its eligibility on an older recovery squash. Set fwd_ready for eligible fast shifts in their normal EX completion cycle. Keep MUL and every non-fast shift ineligible for EX/MEM forwarding. Exclude only fast shifts from id_ex_shift's late-result hazard predicate; genuine load, multiply and other shift dependencies retain their existing single-cycle interlock. Do not mask raw source comparisons or change youngest-writer forwarding priority.\n\nIn MEM, qualify the late-shift result selector with \"'!in.fast_shift. A fast shift uses its captured in.alu_result through the existing ordinary-result arm, while every other shift still uses the original five-level MEM network. Preserve the unified registered wb_result, held WB forwarding after valid clears, exact architectural source values and result selection under both bus stalls. This is essential: without the MEM selection change, early forwarding could differ from retirement. Preserve ALTOPS formulas and standalone ALU full-shift behavior; this fast-path parameter must not alter default arithmetic tests or any M-extension operation.\n\nForecast an unmeasured six percent fitness gain with low confidence. The intended benefit is fewer one-bit/two-bit immediate shift-use bubbles with a much shallower EX shift network than the general barrel shifter. Extra ALU result selection and fast eligibility fanout can reduce Fmax, and the actual frequency of these dependencies can limit cycle savings. No successful prototype or measured speedup is claimed for this final candidate. Measure marker-bounded cycles and the median of all three Gowin placement options; acceptance requires fitness strictly above 234.39814 iter/sec. Preserve the existing nret=1 IO and contiguous retirement order, decoder default-illegal discipline, all alignment traps, predictor/recovery timing, memory handshakes and bounds, canonical CRC/UART validation and every evaluator check.'\"''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'Add a default-disabled, one-bit/two-bit fixed-shift option for the shifts-disabled EX instance. Implement only fixed wiring and one count mux inside the existing shift result arms. Keep default full shifts, all other integer operations and exact ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'Add a fast_shift eligibility bit to the EX/MEM payload, retaining every existing architectural and RVFI field and the nret=1 contract.'},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Enable the narrow fixed-shift ALU option, classify only legal immediate shifts by exactly 1 or 2, register that eligibility and allow their completed results on existing EX/MEM forwarding. Preserve divider metadata, genuine holds, control-only squash, architectural sources, traps and recovery.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Exclude only classified one-bit/two-bit immediate shifts from the late-result shift-use interlock. Preserve raw source hazard comparisons, loads, multiply, all remaining shifts, bus hold priorities and forwarding wiring.'},\n{'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'Use the captured ordinary EX result for fast shifts and retain the original MEM barrel completion for all other shifts. Preserve unified WB registration, retained forwarding data, memory accesses and alignment traps.'},\n{'file':'test/test_ex_stage.py','description':'Update bundle helpers for fast_shift and verify fixed SLLI/SRLI/SRAI results, signed extremes, both forwarded sources, eligible early readiness, reset, holds and squash. Check register shifts and other immediate counts remain late; retain existing divider, address, trap and recovery assertions.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Extend existing exhaustive shift coverage with immediate-count-1/2 dependency chains and ALU/store/address/branch/JALR/M consumers under both bus stalls. Verify exact RVFI operands and results, youngest-writer and x0 behavior, retained WB forwarding and contiguous retirement. Add positive timing assertions that fast shifts need no shift-use bubble and other shifts retain the interlock. Preserve existing correctness checks and adapt only schedule expectations explicitly changed by this optimization.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=88))\nvalidate(h,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert type(h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Written and schema-validated:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
