{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n if '-r6' in r['id'] or '-r7' in r['id']:\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],r.get('fitness'),r.get('fmax_mhz'),r.get('cycles'),r.get('error'))\n  if r.get('category')=='structural':\n   print(r['hypothesis']['motivation'])\n   print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261005-001-r6s0 Shorten the writeback-to-multiplier selection cone 158.39 71.144 4491817 None\nhyp-20261005-002-r6s1 Separate early ALU forwarding from unified late multiply/load writeback 173.2 78.418 4527646 None\nThe champion scores 150.99 iter/sec at 67.824 MHz with 4,491,817 marked\nCoreMark cycles. Its worst path starts at the WB result mux, crosses\nforwarding and the shared multiplier, and ends at the EX/MEM result.\nNo LESSONS.md exists. All five earlier structural attempts were reviewed:\niterative division succeeded, but delayed redirects, blocking partial\nproducts, a separate LSU address and simple MEM multiplication did not\nbeat the current champion. The MEM-multiply attempt reached 67.647 MHz\nand 4,527,646 cycles, scoring 149.41. Its worst path began at EX/MEM's\noperation control and passed through forwarding into EX arithmetic.\nThis suggests that moving the multiplier alone leaves late operation\ndecoding and WB result selection on the operand-delivery path.\n\nRestructure the producer pipeline into an early ordinary-ALU lane and\none late completion lane for multiplication and loads. The late lane\nwrites a single registered architectural WB value. Carry registered\nearly-forwarding readiness with EX/MEM so the forwarding network never\ndecodes the MEM-stage operation to decide whether its result is ready.\nThis is one structural change to the result-production and forwarding\nboundaries. Preserve single issue, nret=1, the existing retirement\nboundary, ordinary ALU/branch latency and the iterative divider.\n\nMove all real MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU arithmetic from EX into a small\ncombinational multiplier in MEM. Feed it from EX/MEM's already captured,\nfully forwarded rs1_val and rs2_val. Retain the champion's single shared\nsignedness-selectable 33-by-33 product: extend rs1 with its sign only for\nMULH/MULHSU and rs2 only for MULH; MUL's low half uses zero extension.\nSelect bits 31:0 for MUL and 63:32 for all high products. Remove real\nmultiplication from the EX ALU result cone. Do not add a blocking\nmultiply FSM,\n…[12763 of 15835 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:74b79ac3a633f486]…\nontiguous RVFI orders, plus exactly zero new\nbubble for immediate shifts by 1/2 and exactly one for all dependent late\nshifts in zero-wait mode. Selftest full retirement cosim against the\nunchanged ISS passed with both bus stalls (115 retirements).\n\nForecast a rounded 8 percent fitness gain with medium confidence. These\nare isolated prototype measurements, not a substitute for the orchestrator's\nimplementation, full correctness gates or fitness evaluation. At the\nmeasured cycle count acceptance requires median Fmax above 83.53761 MHz,\ngiving about 2.33 MHz of margin. The principal risk is another ALU,\nredirect or MEM product-selection path limiting timing after replication,\nor an implementation changing the early-shift eligibility and cycle cost.\nPreserve every evaluator file and every hard invariant, including nret=1\nports, EBREAK-only SYSTEM legality, canonical CRC/UART checks and markers.\n\nhyp-20261005-003-r7s2 Compact micro-BTB with registered training and live successor checks 181.49 77.152 4251084 None\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
