among every process sharing this\n# riscv-formal checkout (the K concurrent agents of a run share it through\n# their worktrees' symlink). The name used to be \"$CORE_NAME-$$\", but an\n# agent's sandboxed shell runs in its own PID namespace where $$ is 2 or 3\n# for every agent, so siblings wrote into one dir and wiped each other's\n# checks mid-SBY (V2 incident 03). mktemp gives a uniqu\n…[1948 of 5020 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6170756e98c754d8]…\n& [ -z \"${CHECKS_CFG:-}\" ] && [ -z \"${1:-}\" ] \\\n   && [ -f \"$CORE_YAML\" ]; then\n    # Pass the path via env var so the python literal stays free of any\n    # bash-substituted strings that could break on quoting / spaces.\n    NRET=$(CORE_YAML=\"$CORE_YAML\" python3 -c '\nimport os, yaml\ntry:\n    d = yaml.safe_load(open(os.environ[\"CORE_YAML\"]).read()) or {}\n    print(int(d.get(\"nret\", 2)))\nexcept Exception:\n    print(2)\n' 2>/dev/null)\n    if [ \"$NRET\" = \"1\" ]; then\n        WRAPPER=\"$SCRIPT_DIR/wrapper_si.sv\"\n        CHECKS_CFG=\"$SCRIPT_DIR/checks_si.cfg\"\n        echo \"[run_all.sh] auto-detected nret=1 from $CORE_YAML; using wrapper_si.sv + checks_si.cfg\"\n    fi\nfi\n\nCHECKS_CFG=\"${CHECKS_CFG:-${1:-$SCRIPT_DIR/checks.cfg}}\"\nWRAPPER=\"${WRAPPER:-$SCRIPT_DIR/wrapper.sv}\"\nif [ ! -f \"$CHECKS_CFG\" ]; then\n    echo \"ERROR: checks.cfg not found at $CHECKS_CFG\"\n    exit 1\nfi\nif [ ! -f \"$WRAPPER\" ]; then\n    echo \"ERROR: wrapper not found at $WRAPPER\"\n110:    dut.io_imemReady.value = 1\n124:        dut.io_imemReady.value = ir\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_36", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r11s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261005-002-r11s1\ntitle: \\\"Decouple fetch from pipeline holds with a transparent two-entry skid buffer\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 199.86 iter/sec at 90.490 MHz and 4,527,646\n  marker-bounded CoreMark cycles. LESSONS.md is absent. Structural r9s1\n  raised Fmax to 103.173 MHz but increased dependency cycles to 5,518,993;\n  r10s1 enlarged bypass/result banking and fell to 77.992 MHz. A private\n  nine-candidate execution matrix in this research turn likewise regressed\n  to 66.049 MHz and is rejected. The most promising remaining structural\n  angle is to overlap independent fetch and downstream pipeline stalls,\n  preserving all current execution and dependency latencies.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a transparent, two-entry instruction skid buffer inside if_stage.\n  Decouple acceptance from the instruction bus from acceptance by ID/EX:\n  fetch can collect available instructions while decode is held by a\n  load/multiply-use dependency, an older stalled memory operation or the\n  blocking divider. When imem subsequently stalls, decode can consume a\n  buffered instruction instead of inserting a fetch bubble. This is one\n  structural conversion of the fetch/decode boundary to an elastic stage.\n  It adds no instruction cache, predictor, memory protocol, issue width\n  or registered stage on the empty-buffer path.\n\n  Retain a separate fetch-PC register driving io_imemAddr. Store ordered\n  {pc,instr} head and tail payloads and a count in the range zero through\n  two. When the count is zero, drive the current bus instruction and fetch\n  PC directly to ID, with availability equal to io_imemReady. When the\n  count is nonzero, drive the head instruction and its recorded PC with\n  availability true independently of current io_imemReady. Raw payload\n  selection depends only on registered occupancy, never on redirect,\n  fetch validity or squash. ID still obtains register operands at actual\n  decode acceptance; do not buffer register values, dependency matches\n  or speculative architectural state with the fetched words.\n\n  Use decode's existing stall_id, not the old combined stall_if, to govern\n  whether the head is consumed. For the raw queue movement controls define\n  available = (count \"'!= 0) || io_imemReady;\n  consume = !stall_id && available;\n  pop = consume && (count != 0);\n  accept = io_imemReady && ((count < 2) || pop);\n  append = accept && !(count == 0 && consume).\n  Thus an empty, accepted fetch passes directly into ID without storage;\n  a held fetch is saved and advances the fetch PC. A full queue accepts\n  another word only when its head is removed on the same edge. When no\n  redirect/reset applies, increment the fetch PC by four only on accept,\n  and update count by append minus pop. Simultaneous pop/append preserves\n  count and replaces the consumed head, with the old tail preceding the\n  newly accepted word when count was two. An unavailable bus contributes\n  no payload regardless of the arbitrary instruction bits it carries.\n\n  Keep reset and EX redirect authoritative over fetch PC and occupancy:\n  reset restores PC zero and an empty queue; redirect sets the target PC\n  and empties both entries, discarding every younger buffered instruction.\n  The existing ID flush kills the wrong-path decode instruction, and the\n  redirecting instruction retains its normal EX/MEM advancement and RVFI\n  successor. Never reinterpret a branch flush as a queue pop while retaining\n  older wrong-path entries, or allow stale entries to survive a redirect.\n  Preserve redirect-over-stall priority, including an instruction-memory\n  stall or a held branch behind older data-memory backpressure.\n\n  Critically, do not include reset qualification, redirect or flush in the\n  raw consume/pop/accept/append combinational equations. Reset is handled\n  by the payload flops'\"' ordinary reset arm; redirect invalidates the count\n  and overrides the PC update, so any simultaneous speculative payload\n  movement is inert. Head/tail payload enables and their data selection\n  must depend only on occupancy, raw imem readiness and stall_id. This keeps\n  the late EX branch comparison out of the wide queue payload D muxes and\n  enables. A first correctly functioning probe with redirect-qualified\n  queue enables had a 17.527 ns branch-to-head-data path and only 81.745\n  MHz in placement option zero; that version is rejected. The proposed\n  payload/control separation removes this newly introduced path without\n  changing accepted instruction sequences or the cycle-saving policy.\n\n  Rewire hazard_unit's fetch-available input to queue-head availability,\n  not raw io_imemReady. Compare the source fields of if_id_w.instr, which\n  now denotes the actual decoded head, rather than io_imemData, which may\n  refer to a younger prefetched word. Keep existing load/multiply-use\n  bubbles, x0 exclusions, divider holds, dmem stall priorities and MEM/WB\n  forwarding retention unchanged. Fetch bus acceptance is capacity-driven\n  and independent of ID holds; decode acceptance and forwarding-match\n  capture remain aligned with the same ID/EX instruction as today.\n\n  Preserve the current EX ALU, shared shifter, dedicated LSU address,\n  MEM multiplier, iterative divider, unified registered WB and nret=1\n  retirement boundary. Both real and ALTOPS builds use the identical\n  buffer and stall policy. Buffered words are not retired or allowed to\n  access dmem until they advance through the normal validated decoder and\n  pipeline. Preserve every top-level port, decoder default-illegal and\n  EBREAK-only SYSTEM rule, alignment trap, architectural source snapshot,\n  memory ordering, contiguous RVFI order and evaluator-side correctness\n  contract. Modify only the specified target RTL and allowed unit tests.\n\n  The isolated initial buffer probe passed full selftest retirement cosim\n  with and without both bus stalls and produced all five canonical CoreMark\n  CRCs, the success literal, both markers, EBREAK and no out-of-bounds access.\n  Its marked cycles were 4,361,564, 3.668 percent below the champion, giving\n  3.808 percent better iterations/cycle. This cycle count is preliminary\n  evidence for the policy, not a successful scored implementation; the\n  corrected payload/control separation must be rerun and formally checked.\n  At that cycle count, the 4.6 percent acceptance threshold of 209.05356\n  iter/sec requires median Gowin Fmax above approximately 91.181 MHz.\n  Forecast an unmeasured eight percent fitness gain with low confidence.\n  The main risk is queue/hazard fanout or occupancy-to-decode timing lowering\n  Fmax enough to consume the cycle saving. Require all three placement\n  options and actual marker-bounded cycles; do not accept the earlier slow\n  probe, infer success from CRCs alone or weaken any test or formal depth.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the transparent two-entry ordered {pc,instr} skid buffer,\n      separate fetch PC and occupancy, capacity-driven bus acceptance and\n      head-driven ID consumption. Expose instruction availability and raw\n      imem readiness. Preserve redirect/reset priority for PC and occupancy,\n      while excluding redirect/flush from all wide payload movement controls.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire raw io_imemReady into fetch, queue-head availability into hazards,\n      and stall_id into head consumption. Compare actual if_id_w instruction\n      source fields instead of younger raw bus data. Remove or explicitly\n      disconnect obsolete stall_if wiring without lint warnings. Preserve\n      stage interfaces downstream, forwarding alignment, IO and channel-0\n      RVFI retirement fields and ordering.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Name/document the fetch readiness input as decoded-head availability,\n      qualifying dependency detection and fetch bubbles with that signal.\n      Retain the existing hazard equations and hold/flush precedence; raw\n      bus readiness must no longer invalidate a queued instruction.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Verify empty fall-through, stalled-head retention, ordered filling,\n      full backpressure, simultaneous enqueue/dequeue, imem unavailability,\n      and redirect/reset discard from every occupancy. Check PC/instruction\n      pairing, no duplication/loss and that no unavailable bus data is saved.\n      Exercise redirect coincident with full-queue movement and ID holds.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all architectural checks and add fetch buffering around\n      load/multiply dependencies, long dmem/divider holds and alternating\n      bus stalls with unrelated unavailable instruction words. Check hazards\n      use the queued head, current RF values are read when ID actually issues,\n      younger stores/DIV/branches are discarded on redirect, and retirement\n      instructions, PC successors, operands, orders and memory writes remain\n      exact. Adapt internal signal observations and intentional cycle-gap\n      assertions only for the buffer's explicitly changed fetch schedule.\n''')\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nx=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()); jsonschema.validate(x,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Validated replacement hypothesis',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
