{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20261005-002-r3s1','hyp-20261005-002-r5s1','hyp-20261005-002-r6s1','hyp-20261005-002-r9s1'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r['id'] in ids:\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],r['fitness'],r['fmax_mhz'],r['cycles'])\n  h=r['hypothesis']; print(h['hypothesis'] if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261005-002-r3s1 Isolate wide multiplication in a registered partial-product unit 117.73 56.203 4773836\nMove all real MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU computation out of the ordinary\ncombinational EX ALU into one blocking registered multiplier. Share\none unsigned 32-by-32 product across all four operations, implemented\nwith four explicitly bounded unsigned 16-by-16 partial products.\nRegister operands before the partial products, and register the\naccumulation and sign-correction boundaries so neither forwarding\nplus multiplication nor multiplication plus a wide accumulation can\nremain in a single clock path. This is one structural change to the\nexecution unit, not a general pipeline or control-flow redesign.\n\nUse a concrete schedule: edge 1 accepts and captures fully forwarded\noperands and operation; edge 2 registers the four 16-by-16 products;\nedge 3 registers the 33-bit sum of the two cross products. MUL can\ncomplete on edge 3, using its low word directly: its low 16 bits are\np00[15:0], and its upper 16 bits are the modulo-2^16 sum of\np00[31:16], p01[15:0] and p10[15:0]. Use a three-input carry-save\ncompression followed by one 16-bit carry-propagate addition to avoid\ntwo serial additions on this frequent path. This result uses the\nsame registered partial products, not a second multiplier.\n\nFor high-half operations, edge 4 assembles and registers the unsigned\nhigh word from p11 and the full cross sum; MULHU completes there.\nSigned-high operations complete on edge 5 with sign correction.\nIn parallel with these stages,\ncompute and register the modulo-2^32 correction term: b when a is\nnegative for MULH or MULHSU, plus a when b is negative for MULH.\nMUL and MULHU use no correction. The signed high result is the\nunsigned high word minus this correction.\nImplement assembly by first forming the 17-bit sum of the low\nproduct's upper half and the cross sum's lower half, then using its\ncarry in the high-word addition. Retain full cross-term carry width.\nKeep the ordinary MUL result independent of these later stages. If\nGowin tim\n…[20686 of 23758 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b34397ab69b94265]…\nmprovement with low confidence. The\nnew architecture is not implemented or measured in this research turn;\nthe preceding probes are negative evidence, not its timing results.\nThe intended benefit is a substantially faster clock because register-\nfile/forwarding and ALU/branch logic occupy different clock paths. The\nprincipal risk is added dependency and control-flow bubbles outweighing\nthat clock gain, or register-file/MEM logic limiting the new clock.\nAt 120 MHz acceptance above 209.05356 iter/sec requires fewer than\napproximately 5,740,156 marked cycles; at 135 MHz it requires fewer than\n6,457,675 cycles. These are tradeoff bounds, not predicted measurements.\nA twelve percent gain would need about 6.03 million cycles at 135 MHz.\nRequire the unchanged formal suite and full retirement cosim, canonical\nCoreMark validation and all three Gowin placement options. Accept only\nthe measured Fmax times marker-bounded iterations/cycle, exceeding the\nchampion by 4.6 percent; do not judge pipeline depth or Fmax alone.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
