{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_39", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r15s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261005-002-r15s1',\n'title':'Mirrored distributed-RAM register file with local reset validity',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 237.23 iter/sec at 97.373 MHz and 4,104,522 marked\nCoreMark cycles. Its integer register file uses 992 individually reset\nflip-flops and wide asynchronous read muxes. Each WB data bit fans out\nto every writable word as well as bypass consumers. A physically smaller\nregister-file topology can reduce that writeback fanout and congestion\nwithout increasing pipeline depth or changing dependency latency.\nLESSONS.md is absent. Prior structural experiments were reviewed:\nblocking multiplication, an additional operand stage, replicated EX banks\nand arithmetic reconstruction all regressed; the two-entry fetch queue\nis retained. A multiplier-preparation probe removed the DSP critical\npath but reached only 95.937 MHz. Four/eight-entry fetch buffers saved\nabout 1.5 percent cycles and worsened timing. Neither is proposed.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace only the flop-based integer register file with two mirrored\nasynchronously read distributed-RAM banks, each supplying one read port.\nBoth copies receive the same synchronous architectural write. This is\none structural change to register storage and operand delivery. Preserve\nthe existing module ports, ID/EX operand registers, writeback boundary,\nforwarding topology, instruction stages and all instruction latencies.\n\nUse Gowin's supported syn_ramstyle=\\\"distributed_ram\\\" attribute on the\nmemory arrays; \\\"distributed\\\" is invalid and silently permits BSRAM\ninference, which a private probe found substantially slower. Use portable\nbehavioral memory RTL with asynchronous reads and synchronous writes so\nVerilator and Yosys elaborate the same architectural storage. Do not use\na vendor-only behavioral shortcut or add a simulation/formal bypass.\nNo synchronous-read BSRAM may absorb the existing ID/EX operand boundary.\n\nKeep the storage arrays free of reset. Instead, maintain one resettable\ninitialized bit per architectural word, shared across the mirrored\ncopies. Synchronous reset clears every bit. A nonzero enabled write when\nreset is low updates both copies and sets only its owning initialized\nbit. Mask unwritten locations to zero on both read ports. Thus every\nregister still observably clears on every reset, including repeated resets\nand reads of stale RAM contents, while the RAM itself retains inferable\nwrite behavior. x0 always reads zero, ignores writes and never gains an\ninitialized bit. Reset wins over memory writes and initialized-bit sets.\n\nPreserve same-cycle write-first behavior exactly: an enabled nonzero WB\nwrite matching either read address supplies w_data immediately, even\nbefore the synchronous edge. Both ports may bypass together. Preserve the\ncurrent combinational behavior during reset as well as post-edge zeroing;\ndo not qualify the read bypass with reset if that changes the original\ninterface. A disabled write never changes RAM or initialized state.\n\nUse local bank selection and mutually exclusive masked-OR terms for RAM\nand WB data so validity and x0 handling do not create a second deep\npriority mux after RAM output. The lower and upper sixteen-register banks\nmay be separately declared per read copy, with local sixteen-bit validity\nvectors and address[4] selecting the owning bank. No extra read register,\nclock phase, new operand stall or decode-address hold is permitted. Keep\nall 32 architectural data bits and stable output behavior for held decode\naddresses. Downstream dmem/divider holds retain existing ID/EX and WB\nforwarding behavior; the register file still writes only valid WB records.\n\nAll ordinary, shift and M-extension arithmetic, ALTOPS formulas, fetch\nqueue/prediction/recovery, trap and decoder discipline, memory requests,\nexact RVFI sources/results/successors, nret=1 ports and contiguous order\nremain architectural. Add no new early retirement or memory speculation.\nChange no evaluator, wrapper, benchmark source or formal configuration.\n\nA private unsplit distributed-RAM prototype used the unchanged vendor\nflow and measured options 0/1/2 at 94.272/100.824/100.824 MHz, median\n100.824 MHz. Option 0 used 2917 logic cells, 955 FFs, 32 LUT-RAM cells,\nfour BSRAMs and two DSPs, versus the champion's 3670 logic cells and\n1916 FFs. The reset-validity implementation passed all eight full\nretirement cosim runs (selftest/random1/2/3, each zero-wait and both bus\nstalls), and canonical CoreMark CRCs/UART/markers/bounds with unchanged\n4,104,522 cycles. That unsplit implementation improves fitness only\nabout 3.5 percent and does not itself clear acceptance. The final local\nbank/read-selection topology must be measured; no qualifying result is\nclaimed by these preliminary numbers.\n\nForecast six percent fitness improvement with low confidence. The\nprincipal risks are LUT-RAM read delay, validity/bypass muxes and the\nunchanged EX arithmetic or multiplier limiting Fmax. Require actual\nmedian Gowin timing and marker-bounded CoreMark cycles. Acceptance needs\nfitness above 248.14258 iter/sec, or median Fmax above approximately\n101.851 MHz at 4,104,522 cycles. Preserve all existing tests and run the\nunchanged single-channel formal gate and retirement-reference cosim.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Replace 31 resettable flop words with mirrored asynchronous-read,\nsynchronous-write distributed-RAM storage, using the supported Gowin\nattribute and local low/high banks. Share resettable per-word initialized\nbits between copies; mask stale/unwritten storage to zero. Preserve\nreset priority, nonzero write eligibility, x0, both write-first bypasses\nand exact original ports/latency. Use mutually exclusive masked-OR read\nselection without inserting another pipeline register.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Retain every existing x0, asynchronous read, synchronous write,\nwrite-first and reset assertion. Cover all 31 locations, both low/high\nbanks, independent and identical read addresses, concurrent dual bypass,\ndisabled writes, reset with write enabled, repeated resets after distinct\nRAM contents, partial repopulation and seeded randomized reference-model\nsequences. Check observable zeros without depending on RAM initialization.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve existing pipeline checks and exercise writes/reads across\nregister-file banks, WB-to-ID bypass and immediate EX/WB dependencies\nthrough ALU, load, shift, MUL and DIV consumers. Include long bus/divider\nholds, younger overwrites, reset after execution, x0 and recovery-squashed\nwrites. Assert exact RVFI sources/results/order and unchanged dependency\nschedule; retain all decoder/trap/forward-progress checks.'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=78))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Rewrote and validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
