{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_48", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r6s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261005-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Register one unified writeback result before operand forwarding\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 150.99 iter/sec at 67.824 MHz with 4,491,817 marked\n  CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path starts in writeback and passes\n  through EX operand forwarding and the shared multiplier. WB currently\n  selects between separately registered load and ALU values using a wide\n  combinational mux; this mux precedes both forwarding and the register\n  file's write-first bypass. No LESSONS.md exists. The structural history\n  shows that blocking multiplication and moving multiplication into MEM\n  did not beat the current champion, while late redirect recovery incurred\n  additional branch cycles. Moving the writeback selection across its\n  existing register boundary attacks the observed path without those costs.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the two architectural-result lanes in MEM/WB with one registered\n  wb_result selected inside MEM. For an advancing instruction, compute\n  wb_result_next = in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result and latch\n  it with the instruction's existing MEM/WB payload. WB drives w_data\n  directly from this register, without a post-register ALU/load mux.\n  This is one structural retiming of the writeback boundary: issue width,\n  execute latency, branch recovery, load-use bubbles, multiplier, divider\n  and the memory interfaces retain their present schedules.\n\n  Use the fully extracted and sign/zero-extended load value for load\n  writeback, and the existing architectural ALU result for ordinary ALU,\n  multiply, completed divide, AUIPC, LUI and JAL/JALR link values. The load\n  selector must come from the EX/MEM instruction being transferred, never\n  the old MEM/WB control. Remove the old MEM/WB read_data/alu_result result\n  lanes and update their consumers, so synthesis cannot reconstruct a mux\n  between two result registers. RVFI's raw mem_rdata remains an independent\n  verification field and must still report the original bus word.\n\n  Preserve ME\n…[2151 of 5223 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5c65539ca4e250ee]…\n it to zero and retain it together with all other forwarding fields\n      during hold_wb while clearing valid only. Preserve bus requests,\n      byte masks, sign extension, alignment traps and raw RVFI mem_rdata.\n  - file: rtl/wb_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive w_data directly from in.wb_result. Preserve valid-qualified\n      register writes and destination routing. Remove the post-register\n      mem_to_reg result mux and update module documentation accordingly.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all current checks and add directed ALU-to-load-to-ALU\n      alternation, all load widths/signs, immediate and separated load\n      consumers, WB-to-ID write-first bypass and a long dmem hold that\n      requires a previously retired load's retained WB result. Include\n      multiply/divide operand consumers and JAL links where appropriate;\n      check exact RVFI source/result values and contiguous orders under\n      both bus stalls, with no duplicate memory effects or retirements.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
