ues in EX1,\n  rather than retaining old operand values through a dependency stall.\n  Keep x0 and the existing write-first WB bypass. In EX1, choose the\n  youngest available registered EX/MEM ordinary result, then registered\n  MEM/WB wb_result, then current register-file data. Capture both resolved\n  architectural source values with the complete instruction metadata in\n  the new EX1/EX2 register. No ALU, barrel shift, branch comparison,\n  address addition or JALR target arithmetic precedes this register.\n\n  EX2 contains the current base ALU, target/link calculations, alignment\n  checks and blocking divider launch/completion logic. Its only operand\n  inputs are the new register's rs1_val and rs2_val; eliminate the EX2\n  forwarding muxes entirely. Keep the dedicated effective-address lane,\n  EX/MEM result and readiness register, existing MEM multiplier and load\n  extraction, unified WB register and retirement boundary. Multiplication\n  consumes EX2's captured operands through the existing EX/MEM fields.\n  DIV/REM requests snapshot these operands and metadata exactly once.\n  Keep real RV32M arithmetic and all exact ALTOPS substitutions.\n\n  A producer still executing in EX2 has no registered result available to\n  EX1. If the buffered consumer needs its nonzero destination, hold that\n  consumer and fetch, let the producer advance, and inject a side-effect-\n  free bubble into EX1/EX2. A load or multiply in EX/MEM is also unavailable\n  until registered WB completion: hold dependent EX1 consumers for the\n  extra cycle. Thus immediate ALU dependencies normally gain one bubble;\n  immediate load/multiply dependencies normally need two rather than one.\n  Independent instructions still issue every clock. Use validated source\n  usage for operand readiness, distinguishing register arithmetic, OP-IMM,\n  loads, stores, branches and JALR from instructions without those sources.\n  Preserve the architectural convention for RVFI source-address/data fields:\n  readiness checks must also cover any raw source value the unchanged\n  cosim contract expects to observe, even when that value is semantically\n  unused. Do not silently substitute arbitrary RVFI source values to avoid\n  an interlock. Prefer conservative raw-source stalls when necessary.\n\n  For each source, an unavailable younger matching writer blocks issue\n  even if an older EX/MEM or WB writer of the same register is available.\n  Exclude x0 producers. Use current registered fwd_ready and WB write\n  eligibility, including alignment-trap suppression. Treat invalid pipeline\n  tokens as inert and preserve effective reg_write/memory controls on all\n  bubbles. Re-reading the register file while EX1 waits captures writes\n  from producers that have already drained; do not rely on stale decoded\n  operand snapshots or a WB source whose instruction has moved away.\n\n  Data-memory backpressure freezes EX/MEM and EX1/EX2 plus the buffered\n  consumer and fetch. MEM/WB clears only retirement valid and retains its\n  producer data and eligibility, as today. Captured EX2 operands remain\n  stable through the complete hold; they no longer depend on a forwarding\n  source surviving that hold. Unfinished division holds EX2 and upstream\n  while older MEM/WB instructions drain once. Division wait emits inert\n  EX/MEM bubbles; completion transfers the saved result once and releases\n  EX2 on that edge. A dependent EX1 instruction cannot capture the divide\n  result until the registered producer is available. Preserve reset\n  cancellation, result backpressure and the current ALTOPS execution rules.\n\n  Resolve branches and jumps in EX2, retaining full-width architectural\n  targets, JAL/JALR links, all branch predicates and alignment traps.\n  Compared with the champion, accepted taken transfers gain one recovery\n  bubble. On a valid EX2 redirect actually advancing past older memory,\n  redirect fetch and squash both younger buffered instructions: decode/EX1\n  and EX1/EX2. Suppress EX1 capture on that edge, including younger stores,\n  branches and DIV/REM requests. The redirecting EX2 instruction advances\n  normally and retires once. Preserve accepted redirect priority over\n  instruction-memory unavailability and dependency bubbles; never drop a\n  target, repeatedly consume a held branch, or squash the older memory op.\n  Keep recovery out of wide operand payload D muxes: clear the new live\n  token and all effective side-effect controls while payloads may capture\n  irrelevant data. No speculation or predictor is added.\n\n  Preserve every core port and the nret=1 channel-0 retirement convention,\n  strictly contiguous RVFI orders, decoder default-illegal and EBREAK-only\n  SYSTEM legality, memory and target traps, memory ordering, all five\n  canonical CoreMark CRCs, UART success text, markers and OOB enforcement.\n  Modify only target RTL and allowed cocotb suites. Do not change formal\n  depths, ALTOPS formulas, evaluator files or the declared retirement width.\n\n  Forecast twelve percent fitness improvement with low confidence. The\n  new architecture is not implemented or measured in this research turn;\n  the preceding probes are negative evidence, not its timing results.\n  The intended benefit is a substantially faster clock because register-\n  file/forwarding and ALU/branch logic occupy different clock paths. The\n  principal risk is added dependency and control-flow bubbles outweighing\n  that clock gain, or register-file/MEM logic limiting the new clock.\n  At 120 MHz acceptance above 209.05356 iter/sec requires fewer than\n  approximately 5,740,156 marked cycles; at 135 MHz it requires fewer than\n  6,457,675 cycles. These are tradeoff bounds, not predicted measurements.\n  A twelve percent gain would need about 6.03 million cycles at 135 MHz.\n  Require the unchanged formal suite and full retirement cosim, canonical\n  CoreMark validation and all three Gowin placement options. Accept only\n  the measured Fmax times marker-bounded iterations/cycle, exceeding the\n  champion by 4.6 percent; do not judge pipeline depth or Fmax alone.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Define the buffered decode-to-EX1 metadata and new EX1/EX2 resolved-\n      operand bundle. Preserve all architectural fields in EX/MEM and WB;\n      include live tokens and complete instruction/control/source metadata.\n  - file: rtl/operand_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add EX1 register-file operand reads, registered-producer forwarding,\n      youngest-writer readiness arbitration and the EX1/EX2 register. Separate\n      payload hold/reset from bubble/redirect side-effect suppression. Capture\n      exact source values only on accepted issue and hold them through stalls.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Retain decode and instruction buffering, but move register-file reads\n      to the buffered EX1 instruction and remove obsolete operand snapshots.\n      Hold the buffered instruction on EX1 dependencies and true downstream\n      freezes; preserve decoder legality and raw instruction independence.\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Move forwarding to EX1. Arbitrate registered EX/MEM early results and\n      retained MEM/WB completion against current register-file values. Return\n      blocked readiness when the youngest matching producer is unavailable;\n      preserve x0 and trap suppression and avoid any EX2 arithmetic bypass.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Coordinate EX1 dependency bubbles, new execution-register holds, fetch\n      and decode buffering, MEM stalls, division holds and EX2 redirect\n      squashes. Distinguish bubbles that let older EX2 instructions drain\n      from complete downstream holds; clear all younger effective controls.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Convert EX to EX2 using only captured operands, eliminating forwarding\n      operand muxes. Preserve ALU/address/target/link computation, traps and\n      divider metadata/results. Gate recovery by actual advancement and\n      freeze complete EX2 operands through MEM/divider backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the added execution boundary, EX1 register-file addressing,\n      forwarding readiness, stage holds and two-stage younger squash. Keep\n      top-level IO, MEM multiplier, unified WB and nret=1 RVFI retirement\n      fields/order unchanged; retire each buffered instruction exactly once.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt structural stage references and intentional cycle-gap assertions\n      while retaining every architectural check. Add ALU/load/MUL/DIV chains,\n      independent one-per-cycle issue, youngest unavailable writer cases,\n      stores/branches/JALR, long simultaneous stalls, dependency re-reads,\n      redirects with two younger instructions and reset cancellation. Assert\n      exact RVFI source/PC/result/trap fields, orders and memory transactions.\n  - file: test/test_ex_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Drive the new captured-operand EX2 interface. Preserve existing address,\n      branch, trap, multiplier metadata and divider correctness assertions;\n      verify stable resolved operands under holds and exactly-once completion.\n  - file: test/test_operand_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Verify both registered producer routes, x0, current RF/WB write-first\n      values, younger-unavailable blocking, alignment-trapped producers,\n      completion availability, bubble/hold/squash precedence and captured\n      source stability. Verify data re-read after a producer drains to RF.\n''')\nPY\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261005-002-r9s1.yaml');h=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schem