e exact selected predicted_next_pc with every accepted fetch\nthrough IF/ID and ID/EX. EX computes the architectural next PC using\nthe existing forwarded operands and alignment/trap rules. Redirect and\nsquash the younger instruction only when that actual next PC differs\nfrom the carried prediction. Compare successors for every valid EX\ninstruction, including ordinary and illegal instructions, so stale\nentries cannot silently skip instructions. A correctly predicted taken\nbranch or jump must neither flush ID nor discard a ready target fetch.\nRVFI pc_wdata always reports the architectural successor, never the\nprediction; JAL/JALR link data remains the original instruction PC+4.\n\nAdvance the predicted fetch\n…[61442 of 64514 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d3aef2ee118de2dc]…\n that edge. Avoid double-training held branches or dropping\nthe owning branch's update. Correct predictions continue to advance\nnormally, including target fetch acceptance under both bus stalls.\n\nThe timing hypothesis is that EX forwarding/comparison ends at local\nrepair flops, while registered repair drives the existing masked PC\nselection and the younger validity/control squashes. Do not introduce\na new EX comparison, target lookup, or combinational branch result\ninto MEM-to-fetch control. Forecast an eight percent fitness gain\nwith low confidence; the extra cycles must be outweighed by median\nGowin Fmax. Acceptance requires fitness greater than 222.4842 iter/sec.\nAt an illustrative two percent cycle increase this requires more than\n95.99 MHz; this is a break-even calculation, not a measured result.\nCheck actual marker-bounded CoreMark cycles, canonical CRC/UART and\nall three vendor placements. Preserve nret=1, external IO, retirement\norders, arithmetic, decoder legality, traps and every evaluator check.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
