{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n print(d['id'],d.get('category'),d.get('outcome'),d.get('fitness'),d.get('fmax_mhz'),d.get('cycles'))\n if d['id']=='hyp-20261005-002-r2s1':\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-8c26bd0 micro_opt improvement 12.12 5.442 4491485\nhyp-20261005-001-r1s0 micro_opt regression 13.23 5.944 4491485\nhyp-20261005-002-r1s1 structural improvement 95.23 42.774 4491817\nhyp-20261005-003-r1s2 predictor regression 13.24 5.557 4197517\nhyp-20261005-001-r2s0 micro_opt improvement 111.35 50.016 4491817\nhyp-20261005-002-r2s1 structural regression 107.09 51.943 4850577\nMove architectural redirect application from combinational EX resolution\nto the existing EX/MEM register. Keep branch comparisons, JAL/JALR target\ncalculation, and alignment checks in EX, but capture their resolved\ncontrol-flow outcome with the instruction. Drive fetch recovery only\nfrom that registered, valid, legal outcome. This is one structural\nchange to the recovery boundary; retain the champion's iterative divider\nand ordinary ALU, register file, memory stages, and nret=1 contract.\n\nUse the registered EX/MEM branch_taken, ctrl.is_jump, ctrl.is_illegal,\nand pc_next fields, or equivalent explicit registered recovery fields.\nA valid aligned taken branch or jump in MEM generates one redirect to\nits recorded architectural successor. Not-taken branches, bubbles, and\nfaulting control transfers generate none. Leave RVFI pc_wdata, JAL/JALR\nlink values, trap semantics, and operand snapshots architectural; delaying\nfetch recovery does not change the retiring instruction's next PC.\nDisconnect every unregistered EX branch decision from IF and ID control\nand from combinational instruction substitution. Avoid adding a direct\nEX-to-fetch bypass that would recreate the original critical path.\n\nDuring registered recovery, redirect the PC and squash both younger\ninstructions: the fetched/decode instruction and the instruction already\nin EX. EX/MEM must receive an invalid, side-effect-free bubble instead\nof the younger EX result. Let the older redirecting instruction advance\nthrough MEM/WB and retire exactly once. Give recovery priority over\nfetch-not-ready, load-use, and younger execute holds. Qualify divider\nlaunches and execute_hold with the EX squash so a wrong-path DIV/REM\nneither launches nor blocks recovery. Similarly prevent a squashed EX\nbranch or jump from generating another redirect, and prevent younger\nloads, stores, and register writes from reaching MEM. Clear all side\neffect controls with invalid bubbles, not just the RVFI valid bit.\n\nPreserve downstream memory backpressure. A branch blocked behind an\nolder MEM load/store remains in EX until it can enter EX/MEM; it cannot\nredirect early or repeatedly. Once that branch occupies EX/MEM it has\nno data-memory request, so it advances normally and recovery fires once.\nInstruction-memory stalls must not discard its recorded target. Keep\nthe existing MEM/WB forwarding retention during dmem stalls and divider\ncompletion behavior, including the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS execution path.\n\nThis adds one recovery bubble per taken branch or jump while leaving\nnon-control instruction throughput and load-use latency unchanged.\nThe expected benefit is a materially higher Fmax from removing the\nEX-comparator-to-fetch-squash-to-decode control chain. Measure the actual\ncycle cost under the same approximately 22 percent stalls on both buses.\nSuccess requires (new median Fmax / 42.774 MHz) times\n(4,491,817 / new marked cycles) greater than 1.046, rather than an Fmax\nimprovement alone. The principal risks are the added branch bubbles and\na new critical path through the ordinary ALU or register-file read