every accepted fetch\nthrough IF/ID and \n{'id': 'hyp-20261005-003-r2s2', 'title': 'Timing-aware tagged branch prediction on the iterative-divider core', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 109.55, 'fmax_mhz': 45.983, 'cycles': 4197447, 'lut4': 3059, 'error': None}\nAdd a single 64-entry direct-mapped tagged branch-target predictor to\nthe CURRENT iterative-divider core. Index by fetch PC[7:2], compare all\nremaining PC bits, and store an aligned full-width target, valid bit,\nconditional/unconditional bit, and two-bit saturating direction counter.\nA conditional hit predicts taken when its counter MSB is set; an\nunconditional hit predicts its last resolved target. A m\n…[23074 of 26146 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:63da2d7d34ad659e]…\ntargets, a valid bit and a two-bit\nsaturating counter per entry. Every hit uses the counter MSB, including\nJAL; a new JAL entry starts weakly taken and repeated JAL outcomes\nreinforce taken. No conditional/unconditional type bit is stored.\nMisses predict PC+4. Leave JALR unpredicted. Allocate\nonly after legal aligned taken direct transfers, initialize new\nentries weakly taken, and train both outcomes on hits.\nNot-taken misses must not evict useful entries. Cache the branch\ndestination independently of its outcome, never PC+4 on a not-taken\nupdate. Reset valid bits only. Use independent constant-index entry\nregisters, local one-hot hit qualification and balanced target OR\nselection. Lookup uses the registered PC without imem decoding.\n\nExploit the actual front-end topology: IF/ID is combinational and\nID/EX is the sole instruction register before EX. After a fetch is\naccepted into ID/EX, the fetch PC itself holds that instruction's\nselected successor. If EX is blocked by an older memory operation or\nunfinished\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
