{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('\\\\nID',r['id'])\n  print(h.get('hypothesis','')[:6000] if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\nID hyp-20261005-003-r1s2\nAdd one compact next-PC predictor: a 64-entry direct-mapped tagged BTB\nindexed by fetch PC[7:2], with full remaining PC tags, aligned 32-bit\ntargets, a conditional/unconditional bit, and a two-bit saturating\ndirection counter per entry. A matching unconditional entry predicts\nits last resolved target; a conditional entry predicts its target only\nwhen the counter MSB is set. Misses predict PC+4. Initialize a newly\nallocated conditional entry weakly toward its resolved outcome. Train\nboth branch outcomes, learn JAL/JALR targets, and refresh indirect\ntargets on resolution. Do not add a return stack or global history.\n\nLookup uses only the registered fetch PC, not an instruction-memory\ndecode or a forwarding-dependent operand. Store full targets rather\nthan adding branch immediates on the fetch critical path. Reset entry\nvalid bits, and keep invalid payloads unobservable; avoid resetting the\ntag/target arrays so the vendor can infer distributed storage. Keep the\npredictor small enough that its lookup and PC mux remain well below the\nexisting divider path. Do not change arithmetic or pipeline depth.\n\nCarry the exact selected predicted_next_pc with every accepted fetch\nthrough IF/ID and ID/EX. EX computes the architectural next PC using\nthe existing forwarded operands and alignment/trap rules. Redirect and\nsquash the younger instruction only when that actual next PC differs\nfrom the carried prediction. Compare successors for every valid EX\ninstruction, including ordinary and illegal instructions, so stale\nentries cannot silently skip instructions. A correctly predicted taken\nbranch or jump must neither flush ID nor discard a ready target fetch.\nRVFI pc_wdata always reports the architectural successor, never the\nprediction; JAL/JALR link data remains the original instruction PC+4.\n\nAdvance the predicted fetch PC only when the current instruction is\naccepted: imem ready, no load-use hold, no dmem hold, and no recovery.\nDefine resolve_fire as a valid EX instruction advancing to EX/MEM.\nQualify bo\n…[37307 of 40379 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:95da0e0eb774ac4d]…\n using ordinary payload reset/hold\nlogic; ignore it when invalid. A correctly predicted direct transfer\nadmits a ready target fetch without squashing it. Gate recovery with\na valid EX instruction actually advancing to EX/MEM, excluding dmem\nfreezes and unfinished division. Accepted repair overrides imem and\nload-use holds. Divider result transfer participates in normal\nfall-through resolution, while preserving its saved operands/results.\n\nTrain direction counters from a small separate EX/MEM-aligned record:\nfour-bit branch index, one-bit actual outcome, and a valid event bit.\nCapture only legal aligned conditional branches on EX advancement;\nhold the record with EX/MEM and clear its event on bubbles/divider\nwaits. Consume it once on MEM advancement. No full PC, target, decoded\ninstruction or predictor state enters div_metadata_q. Thus forwarded\nbranch comparison drives only local update flops, never distributed\ntable writes in the same cycle. Use constant-index counter registers,\nlocal write enables and bala\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
